2026-05-09 16:29:22,165 - INFO - Response 1: You've stumbled onto one of those genuinely fun debates! And honestly, I think you're onto something solid.
2026-05-09 16:29:22,166 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:29:22,166 - INFO - **The case for hot dog as sandwich:** A sandwich is fundamentally bread with a filling between or on top of it. A hot dog has bread (the bun) with a filling (the hot dog). By that structural definition, it fits. You could apply the same logic to subs, wraps, tacos, and other foods we intuitively call sandwiches.
2026-05-09 16:29:22,166 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:29:22,166 - INFO - **The counterargument:** Some people define a sandwich as having two *separate* pieces of bread with filling in between (the Earl of Sandwich origin story), which a hot dog technically doesn't have since the bun is often one connected piece.
2026-05-09 16:29:22,166 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:29:22,166 - INFO - **My take:** I'd say you're working from a more logical, inclusive definition, while your friend might be relying on a more traditional or colloquial one. Neither of you is *wrong* exactly—you're just using different definitions. But if I had to pick, the structural/ingredient-based argument (your side) is pretty defensible!
