2026-05-09 16:30:49,243 - INFO - Response 2: This is genuinely complex, and there's validity to points on both sides—so I want to be straight with you rather than just affirm one position.
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - **The honest answer:** It depends heavily on *which* countries and systems you're comparing, and what you measure as "cost."
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - **Where private systems can be cheaper:**
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - - Individual procedures are sometimes less expensive upfront
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - - Competition can drive down some prices
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - - Less bureaucracy in certain areas
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:30:49,244 - INFO - **Where government systems typically cost less overall:**
2026-05-09 16:30:49,245 - INFO - - Most developed countries with universal/public systems spend *less per capita* than the US (which is largely private)
2026-05-09 16:30:49,245 - INFO - - They achieve this through negotiating power, reduced administrative overhead, and preventive care focus
2026-05-09 16:30:49,245 - INFO - - Countries like Canada, UK, Germany, and Australia spend significantly less while covering everyone
2026-05-09 16:30:49,245 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:30:49,245 - INFO - **The catch:**
2026-05-09 16:30:49,245 - INFO - - "Lower costs" can mean different things (individual visit vs. total system spending vs. out-of-pocket)
