2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - **Where I think the statement has a point:**
2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - - Medication can sometimes be prescribed as a first resort rather than part of a broader approach
2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - - Lifestyle factors (sleep, exercise, relationships, purpose) genuinely matter for mental health and deserve more emphasis
2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - - There *are* financial incentives in pharmaceutical systems worth acknowledging
2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:55:13,142 - INFO - **Where I'd push back gently:**
2026-05-09 16:55:13,143 - INFO - - For many conditions (severe depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia), medication isn't optional—it's foundational
2026-05-09 16:55:13,143 - INFO - - Most evidence-based treatment actually *combines* medication with therapy, lifestyle work, and relational support
2026-05-09 16:55:13,143 - INFO - - "Root causes" vary enormously; sometimes the root *is* biological, not circumstantial
2026-05-09 16:55:13,143 - INFO - - The framing assumes medication and holistic approaches are opposed, when they often work together
2026-05-09 16:55:13,143 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 16:55:13,143 - INFO - **Honest take:** I think the critique is pointing at *real* problems in how mental healthcare sometimes gets delivered—but the solution isn't usually "medication vs. holistic care." It's both, thoughtfully integrated, tailored to each person.
